# Introduction

In many companies, manuals, operations guides and architecture descriptions are still written in word processors. Files are passed around by email, changes are hard to trace, and by the third release at the latest, nobody knows which version is the right one.

*Docs as code* applies proven software development practices to documentation: texts are kept as plain text files under version control, reviewed just like source code and automatically turned into polished output formats.{% footnote %}The term was popularized largely by the Write the Docs community.{% /footnote %}

> Good documentation keeps pace with the product – having looked good once is not enough.

## Methodology

This report is based on the {% $survey %} of 214 technical writers, developers and product managers at companies with 50 to 2,000 employees. In addition, we conducted twelve in-depth interviews.

{% callout type="note" title="How to read this report" %}
Chapter 2 describes the current state, chapter 3 contains our recommendations. If you are short on time, start with the [summary of recommendations](recommendations.mdoc#at-a-glance).
